            0.724,
            0.244
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Table 11.2 reveals that the resistivity of an alloy is generally higher than that of its constituent metals. Alloys do not oxidise (burn) readily at high temperatures. For this reason, they are commonly used in electrical heating devices, like electric iron, toasters etc. Tungsten is used almost exclusively for filaments of electric bulbs, whereas copper and aluminium are generally used for electrical transmission lines.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.102,
            0.279,
            0.719,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table_caption",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.099,
                0.249,
                0.642,
                0.267
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Table 11.2 Electrical resistivity* of some substances at "
                },
                {
                  "type": "equation_inline",
                  "content": "2 0 ^ { \\circ } \\mathbf { C }"
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "table_body",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.102,
                0.279,
                0.719,
                0.607
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_8_table_4.jpg",
              "content": "<table><tr><td></td><td>Material</td><td>Resistivity (Ω m)</td></tr><tr><td>Conductors</td><td>Silver Copper Aluminium Tungsten Nickel Iron</td><td><eq>1 . 6 0 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq> <eq>1 . 6 2 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq> <eq>2 . 6 3 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq> <eq>5 . 2 0 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq> <eq>6 . 8 4 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq></td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Chromium Mercury</td><td><eq>1 0 . 0 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq> <eq>1 2 . 9 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq></td></tr><tr><td>Alloys</td><td>Manganese Constantan (alloy of Cu and Ni) Manganin (alloy of Cu, Mn and Ni) Nichrome</td><td><eq>9 4 . 0 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 8 }</eq> <eq>1 . 8 4 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 6 }</eq> <eq>4 9 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 6 }</eq> <eq>4 4 \\times 1 0 ^ { - 6 }</eq></td></tr></table>"
            },
